From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Burke Cates <burke.cates@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 16:22:46 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] NOT-FOR-UPSTREAM: ASoC: qcom: q6apm-dai: don't requeue capture buffers of a
 suspending graph

The DATA_READ_DONE handler queues the next read whenever the stream is
RUNNING, and the SUSPEND trigger leaves the state RUNNING while it stops
the graph. A graph that is being stopped hands every read buffer
straight back, so the handler and the DSP ping-pong the capture buffers
on the GPR receive work, and the reply to the graph stop does not get
through:

  qcom-apm gprsvc:service:2:1: CMD timeout for [1001003] opcode

On the ASUS Zenbook A16, with the internal microphones open in PipeWire
across s2idle, ftrace counted about 43,000 graph callbacks a second for
the whole 5.4 s from the capture front end's GRAPH_STOP until the
timeout.

Mark the stream suspended before the stop and don't queue reads while it
is, and queue the period buffers again after RESUME has restarted the
graph, as prepare does.

Assisted-by: Claude Code (claude-opus-5-5)
---
Not-for-upstream: local workaround for one laptop, not reviewed for
  submission; use only for local testing.

 sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm-dai.c | 28 ++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm-dai.c b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm-dai.c
index 6c58d71fae5c..9f3f32326ac2 100644
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm-dai.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm-dai.c
@@ -271,7 +271,13 @@ static void event_handler(uint32_t opcode, uint32_t token, void *payload, void *
 		break;
 	case APM_CLIENT_EVENT_DATA_READ_DONE:
 		queue_work(system_highpri_wq, &prtd->period_work);
-		if (prtd->state == Q6APM_STREAM_RUNNING)
+		/*
+		 * A graph being stopped for system suspend hands every read
+		 * buffer straight back; queueing it again would loop until the
+		 * stop times out. RESUME queues the buffers again.
+		 */
+		if (prtd->state == Q6APM_STREAM_RUNNING &&
+		    !READ_ONCE(prtd->graph_suspended))
 			q6apm_read(prtd->graph);
 
 		break;
@@ -473,8 +479,16 @@ static int q6apm_dai_trigger(struct snd_soc_component *component,
 	case SNDRV_PCM_TRIGGER_RESUME:
 		/* Restart the graph stopped for system suspend. */
 		if (prtd->graph_suspended) {
-			prtd->graph_suspended = false;
 			ret = q6apm_graph_start(prtd->graph);
+			WRITE_ONCE(prtd->graph_suspended, false);
+			/* The stop returned every read buffer: queue them again. */
+			if (!ret && substream->stream == SNDRV_PCM_STREAM_CAPTURE &&
+			    !q6apm_is_graph_in_push_pull_mode(prtd->graph)) {
+				int i;
+
+				for (i = 0; i < runtime->periods; i++)
+					q6apm_read(prtd->graph);
+			}
 		}
 		break;
 	case SNDRV_PCM_TRIGGER_START:
@@ -492,8 +506,14 @@ static int q6apm_dai_trigger(struct snd_soc_component *component,
 		 * DDR awake) through system suspend; stop it until RESUME.
 		 */
 		if (prtd->state == Q6APM_STREAM_RUNNING && !prtd->graph_suspended) {
-			if (!q6apm_graph_stop(prtd->graph))
-				prtd->graph_suspended = true;
+			/*
+			 * Set before the stop, so that the event handler stops
+			 * queueing capture buffers. q6apm_graph_stop() drops
+			 * the start count even when the DSP does not answer, so
+			 * RESUME starts the graph again either way.
+			 */
+			WRITE_ONCE(prtd->graph_suspended, true);
+			q6apm_graph_stop(prtd->graph);
 		}
 		break;
 	case SNDRV_PCM_TRIGGER_PAUSE_PUSH:
